 Review comment:
   I understand but the point of this PR is to maintain a similar speed of mmap without incurring into the time-to-safepoint issue; that's why the logic has changed.

   The original implementation (pre-mmap) was instantiating for each file a giant direct `ByteBuffer`, impacting on the overall native memory footprint and causing a long context switch while making the kernel fill that buffer from its OS (file) page cache.
   It has been solved using mmap, to avoid allocations and copies, but `MappedByteBuffer::get(byte)` could pause the JVM for long time: that's why I'm addressing both the issues at once: memory footprint and long time to safepoints (to save memory).

